//! `src/util/integer.h`: the checked integer conversions and overflow-checked arithmetic
//! libgit2 names `git__is_*` and `git__*_overflow`, ported to Rust.
//!
//! The header has no translation unit and no state: its six `GIT_INLINE` functions and six
//! macros are native Rust here, reached by no C, so the module has no unsafe regions and no
//! exports. The header stays, whole, for the C that still includes it, `git2_util.h`, which
//! every translation unit of libgit2 includes, and `vector.c`: that C expands the macros and
//! inlines the functions' bodies from it, as porting's Macros and Static inline functions
//! sections keep a header's items, and none of it calls the Rust.
//!
//! # The range checks
//!
//! Each `git__is_<type>(p)` converts `p` to `<type>` and back, and reports whether the round
//! trip kept it. Their C callers pass `int64_t`, `uint64_t` (`git_object_size_t`) and
//! `size_t` values alike through the one parameter, converted at the call. The ports take
//! each value at its own type and report whether it is in `<type>`'s range, which is what
//! their documentation states and what every caller asks. The round trip departs from that
//! range only where the conversion keeps the width, and each such departure is a C defect
//! the ports do not copy:
//!
//! - `git__is_ssizet` converts `size_t` to `ssize_t` and back, which keeps every value: it
//!   never returns false, so its callers' guards (`p_read`, `p_write`,
//!   `git_futils_readbuffer_fd`) accept a count past `SSIZE_MAX`. [`fits_isize`] refuses it.
//! - `git__is_sizet` and `git__is_ulong` on a target whose `size_t` and `unsigned long` are
//!   64 bits wide, as this build's are, keep every `int64_t`, a negative one included: a
//!   negative `off_t` or configured size passes as a huge one (`pack-objects.c`'s
//!   `config_get` of `pack.deltaCacheSize`). [`fits_usize`] and [`fits_c_ulong`] refuse
//!   negative values; for an unsigned 64-bit value, the case of the round trip through a
//!   negative `int64_t`, they give C's answer.
//! - `git__is_int` of a `uint64_t` or `size_t` past `INT64_MAX` sees the negative `int64_t`
//!   the call converts it to, and accepts `UINT64_MAX` as `-1`. [`fits_c_int`] refuses it.
//!
//! # The overflow checks
//!
//! Each `git__<op>_<type>_overflow(out, one, two)` returns true on overflow, and otherwise
//! false with `one <op> two` in `*out`. The ports return that result, `None` on overflow, as
//! the standard library's `checked_*` operations do, over which they are written. On this
//! build, gcc or clang, the macros expand to the compilers' `__builtin_*_overflow` intrinsics
//! (`__builtin_uaddl_overflow` and `__builtin_umull_overflow` for `size_t`, `unsigned long`
//! here). The header's other branches, Microsoft's `<intsafe.h>` functions under `_MSC_VER`
//! and the `GIT_INLINE` fallbacks for a compiler with neither, report the same overflows and
//! results, but for `git__multiply_int64_overflow`'s fallback, which the build takes only
//! where neither applies or under clang on a 32-bit target (`GIT_ARCH_32`): as its comment
//! and `tests/util/integer.c`'s edge cases record, it reports `INT64_MIN * -1` and
//! `-1 * INT64_MIN` as fitting, with `INT64_MIN` as the product, and `INT64_MIN * 1` and
//! `1 * INT64_MIN` as overflowing; [`checked_mul_i64`] gives this build's answer, which is
//! the mathematical one.
